Hello All,

This is an update to you on this matter to give perspective.  At the end ar=
e some guidelines on how to discuss this topic with customers when necessar=
y.

As some of you are more aware than others, our molded sewer joints require =
higher insertion forces/effort than our competitors and has become a factor=
 in sales.  That our joint is tighter than our competitors is not a new dev=
elopment.  That it has become a factor in growing sales today is apparent a=
nd in some cases threatens current sales.  Note this effort includes the sp=
igots.

The program has these elements:

1.       Characterize the performance of our current design "at the extreme=
s".

2.       Characterize what is currently manufactured.

3.       Identify the changes required to improve the ease of installation =
while meeting all other needs.

4.       Implement the changes.

A parallel effort that may allow some relief in the interim is:  Not meant =
to take the place of the design upgrade.

5.       Research the potential for different installation instructions tha=
t could improve the customer experience.

6.       Implement new installation instructions and videos if useful.  The=
re are tricks to installation that we have never broadly publicized in a pe=
rmanent form.  As contractor staff transitions due to age, these can be los=
t especially given upheaval of these firms in 2008.

Schedule:  This will take several months.
The first thing we have done is identify and acquire the staff required to =
conduct what could be an extensive dimensional measurement campaign and to =
identify and acquire the equipment needed to perform standardized installat=
ion force tests on our product and on our competitors.  A change to the des=
ign will be required assuming this continues to be confirmed not to be QC p=
roblem.  The change will be one or a combination of the following.

7.       Groove dimension changes

8.       Gasket dimension changes

9.       Gasket material changes

Other points that will factor in this effort are:

10.   A looser joint challenges the sealing capability at the other extreme=
 of the tolerances

11.   And thus the impact this change will have on the reliability of seal =
of the product line.

12.   Reaction of customers who value the tight joint for the confidence in=
 sealing it gives them

The quickest route to a change is an hypothetical adjustment to the gasket =
design.  This would require changes to our gasket molds or possibly new mol=
ds.  There will be the issue of existing inventory that may or may not exte=
nd the time to commercialization depending on how we can choreograph the tr=
ansition.

Conversations with customers:
Conversations with customers that speak to our efforts to optimize the desi=
gn for insertion ease should not speak to a particular change such as groov=
e dimensions or tolerances.

A.      You need only remark that changes to the joint design that are effe=
ctive in all respects will be implemented to respond to their concerns and =
make insertion forces acceptable.

B.      With respect to schedule you can say that our best current estimate=
 is that it will take a minimum of several months because of the tooling ch=
anges that are likely and resolution of current inventory.

C.      Enlist contractors, distributors, and municipalities to test drive =
prototype samples to get market feedback on potential solutions but not yet=
.

This is an open ended problem meaning we don't know what we don't know.  As=
 we get into it we will learn more that will allow us to refine our underst=
anding of schedule.  If it can be done quickly we will do it quickly.  As s=
chedule becomes more refined you will be informed.

j

